analysis:
The PR changes only CI test harnesses: scripts/test-darkbloom-route.py and scripts/test-provider-ci-workflow.py. It touches no provider runtime code, coordinator protocol, keys, attestation or cache code, so none of the threat-model assets or boundaries (TB-002, TB-003, TB-007, TB-009, TB-011, TB-012, TB-013) are directly affected.

The head side of test-darkbloom-route.py extends ROUTED to the macOS CI jobs (test-provider, test-provider-summary, test-provider-sdk, and the cluster jobs). It adds per-shard lane naming for SHARDED jobs via requested_lanes and job_lanes, and it removes the base requirement that CI macOS jobs keep literal Blacksmith runners.

The security-relevant controls remain asserted by the head tests:
- routed jobs must have explicit read-only permissions with no id-token (lines 532-548);
- routed jobs must not use secrets, environment, services or container;
- routed jobs must have a timeout of at most 60 minutes;
- the router stays isolated, with permissions {} and no checkout, secrets or uses (lines 462-490);
- UNROUTED release and credentialed workflows keep literal blacksmith runners and must not contain self-hosted labels or reference the route (lines 567-579);
- only the exact profile label arrays are accepted from the plane;
- fork PRs never contact the plane;
- plane text injection into workflow commands and markdown is blocked.

One observation, not a finding: the base test pinned CI macOS jobs to literal runners. The head intentionally moves them to plane-routed self-hosted macOS runners. Those jobs are test jobs, outside the release and credentialed set, and the head tests still enforce no secrets and read-only permissions. Whether the actual ci.yml and darkbloom-route.yml files match these assertions is not visible here, because the workflow YAML is not in the supplied units.

The unit for scripts/test-provider-ci-workflow.py (8cff4d4155b087d6:0) carried only metadata and a truncated patch excerpt. It shows the test now expects fromJSON(needs.route.outputs.routes) runs-on for test-provider and the other macOS lanes instead of a literal Blacksmith runner. Its full content was not available, so a residual coverage limit applies. The next pass should confirm that the actual ci.yml jobs keep read-only permissions, that no secret-bearing steps run on the self-hosted routed runners for PR code, and that the fork guard holds.
